Keys: share sweet_spmi.c's arbiter lock, drop impossible values, debounce two polls (phantom presses).
User report (boot 1135): the phone presses power by itself (wakes, or locks while in use) and sometimes volume.
The kernel log of all boots so far (SWEET KEYS key=K down=1 pon=.. gpio=..): of 518 power, 245 volume-down and
380 volume-up presses, 226 / 123 / ~283 carried register values PON/GPIO RT_STS cannot hold (PON 0x1d/0x1f/0xc3,
GPIO 0x50/0x80/0x3c/0xc4/0x70 ...: charger, fuel gauge and WLED register contents), beside 758 runs of failed
reads, many DROPPED (status 0x9). sweet_keys.c issued its observer commands without the lock sweet_spmi.c holds
for the battery, RTC, reboot and WLED-fade transactions (the fade runs exactly at screen wake and lock): commands on
different APIDs of the one EE observer still interfere, as Linux's single pmic_arb lock assumes. Now:
 - keys_read holds sp_lock (sweet_spmi_bus_lock/unlock) for the whole transaction;
 - a PON read with bits above 0-2, or a GPIO read with bits above 0, is ignored (logged every 100);
 - a key changes state only on the second poll in a row that shows the change (20-40 ms).

diff --git a/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.h b/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.h
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.h
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.h
@@ -19,6 +19,12 @@
 int sweet_spmi_prepare_write(unsigned sid, unsigned addr);
 /* Reset time: no more sleeping locks or mappings in this module. */
 void sweet_spmi_atomic(void);
+/*
+ * sweet_keys.c's own observer reads: hold the lock this module holds around each PMIC arbiter transaction, so the
+ * two never have commands in flight at once. 1 = taken (pass it back to unlock), 0 = not (no SPMI). Thread context.
+ */
+int sweet_spmi_bus_lock(void);
+void sweet_spmi_bus_unlock(int taken);
 /* Poco F1 wall clock from the PM8998 RTC (patch 0245). Thread context. get: Unix seconds, 0 none. */
 uint64_t sweet_rtc_get(void);
 void sweet_rtc_set(uint64_t unix_secs);
diff --git a/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c b/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c
--- a/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/sweet_spmi.c
@@ -2,7 +2,9 @@
  * Battery-only adaptation of input/sweet_keys.c, observer EXT_READL.
  * Reads go through the observer and issue one-byte read commands.
  * No interrupt clear, peripheral enable or charger config.
- * Battery APIDs are disjoint from the existing keys' 008/4c1 APIDs.
+ * Battery APIDs are disjoint from the existing keys' 008/4c1 APIDs, but the keys
+ * still take sp_lock for their reads (sweet_spmi_bus_lock): disjoint APIDs were
+ * not enough, key reads returned battery register values (patch 1136).
  *
  * The one write path (sweet_spmi_write, Poco F1 only) exists for the
  * backlight fade (SweetFramebuffer.cpp, sweetfb_blfade), the reboot reason
@@ -92,6 +94,27 @@
 static int sweet_spmi_init_locked(void);
 
 /*
+ * Up to 1135 sweet_keys.c issued its observer commands without sp_lock. Its logged key presses carried values the
+ * PON/GPIO status registers cannot hold (PON 0x1d/0x1f/0xc3, GPIO 0x50/0x80/0x3c/0xc4: charger, fuel gauge and WLED
+ * contents) beside DROPPED (0x9) statuses: commands on different APIDs of one EE's observer still interfere, as the
+ * single pmic_arb lock in Linux's spmi-pmic-arb.c assumes. Those reads match the phantom power/volume presses.
+ */
+int
+sweet_spmi_bus_lock(void)
+{
+	if (sweet_spmi_init() != 0) {
+		return 0;
+	}
+	return sp_take() ? 1 : 0;
+}
+
+void
+sweet_spmi_bus_unlock(int taken)
+{
+	sp_put(taken != 0);
+}
+
+/*
  * The battery driver and the framebuffer (WLED fade) both start this from
  * their own threads: serialize.
  */
